<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    The SDK's own test suite, run OUTSIDE Unity with `dotnet test`. It is the same files the Unity
    Test Runner executes from BH.SDK.Tests.asmdef - not a copy, not a subset - which is the whole
    reason it is worth having: a red test here means the engine-free build is broken, and it can be
    seen on a machine with no Editor installed.

    THE INCLUDE IS RECURSIVE, AND IT HAS TO BE. It was `*.cs` - the folder ROOT alone - so
    Tests/Rules (49 files, one per rule attribute) and Tests/Services (7, the package and crypto
    round trips) compiled in Unity and were silently absent here. Nothing failed: the run simply
    reported 454 tests that passed, which is exactly the shape of a suite that has stopped covering
    what it claims to. An asmdef governs every subfolder under it and this file must match that, so
    a new Tests/ subfolder needs no edit here - only the two build outputs are excluded, since
    Directory.Build.props redirects them INTO this tree as bin~/obj~.

    net8.0 rather than netstandard2.1, because a test project is an executable host: the framework
    is what VSTest loads it into, and it says nothing about what the LIBRARY targets. The library
    under test stays netstandard2.1 through the ProjectReference.

    THE UNITY-SIDE ASMDEF AND THIS FILE MUST BE KEPT IN STEP BY HAND. Unity's asmdef enumerates
    references and constrains itself to UNITY_INCLUDE_TESTS; this file enumerates packages. Adding a
    dependency in one and not the other produces a suite that passes in one place and does not
    compile in the other.

    UnityExtensions/Tests is deliberately absent: those cover the Unity-dependent half of the SDK
    (RectTransform2D, AvatarMovement) and cannot run without an engine.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>9.0</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>disable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>disable</ImplicitUsings>
    <AssemblyName>BH.SDK.Tests</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>BH.SDK</RootNamespace>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <AppendTargetFrameworkToOutputPath>false</AppendTargetFrameworkToOutputPath>
    <EnableDefaultCompileItems>false</EnableDefaultCompileItems>
    <GenerateAssemblyInfo>false</GenerateAssemblyInfo>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="**\*.cs" Exclude="bin~\**\*.cs;obj~\**\*.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\BH.SDK.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    NUnit 3, matching com.unity.ext.nunit - Unity has never shipped NUnit 4, and its [Author] /
    [Category] attributes plus the constraint model are what every fixture here is written against.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="NUnit" Version="3.14.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="NUnit3TestAdapter" Version="4.6.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.11.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="Newtonsoft.Json" Version="13.0.3" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
